  10: 
  11:     Input structure:
  12:     - Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers).
  13:     - Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.
  14: 
  15:     Rule:
  16:     - Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color.
  17:     - Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position.
  18:     - Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.
  19: 
  20:     Key insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor.
  21:     """
  22:     g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)
  23:     H, W = g.shape
  24: 
  25:     # --- find all uniform-border rectangles (brute force, small grid) ---
  26:     def interior_bg(rect: tuple[int, int, int, int]) -> int:
  27:         r0, c0, r1, c1 = map(int, rect)
  28:         inner = g[int(r0) + 1 : int(r1), int(c0) + 1 : int(c1)]
  29:         return int(Counter(map(int, inner.flatten())).most_common(1)[0][0])
  30: 
